Ladybird sends, for each User-Agent preset, the TLS ClientHello and
HTTP/2 preface of the browser the preset names, through the profiles
curl-impersonate maintains. Each browser's own shape comes from its TLS
stack. Chromium's comes from BoringSSL as net/socket/
ssl_client_socket_impl.cc configures it: SSL_CTX_set_grease_enabled(),
SSL_set_permute_extensions() and SSL_set_enable_ech_grease(). Gecko's
comes from NSS: security/manager/ssl/nsNSSComponent.cpp sets the cipher
preferences with SSL_CipherPrefSetDefault() and turns on
SSL_ENABLE_DELEGATED_CREDENTIALS, and nsNSSIOLayer.cpp sets the version
range with SSL_VersionRangeSet(). WebKit's on Apple platforms comes
from the system's networking stack, which NetworkSessionCocoa.mm leaves
at its defaults. None of them impersonates another browser; Ladybird
borrows their shapes because its own handshake would be libcurl's,
which bot managers know by its fingerprint.
